WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.195
This is NEXUS, the architecture workbench.

00:00:04.895 --> 00:00:09.212
In the next five minutes, you'll see the problem it solves, why it

00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:14.053
matters to the business, and how an architect designs a system, breaks it,

00:00:14.053 --> 00:00:17.716
and corrects it, with AI assisting, and people deciding.

00:00:19.216 --> 00:00:25.713
Here's the problem. Architecture is reviewed on paper, and tested in production. Standards

00:00:25.713 --> 00:00:30.117
live in documents, so violations surface late, or not at all.

00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:36.666
Capacity and failure are estimated in spreadsheets. The real bottleneck, and what a

00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:40.118
zone outage actually does, is found by customers.

00:00:40.818 --> 00:00:46.483
And decisions lose their reasons. Months later, nobody can say which version was

00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:47.687
approved, or why.

00:00:49.187 --> 00:00:55.186
For the business, resilience is now a board level question. Downtime costs revenue

00:00:55.186 --> 00:01:00.600
and trust, and regulators expect recovery to be demonstrated, not assumed.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.354
Hybrid and multi cloud multiply the ways a system can fail. And senior

00:01:06.354 --> 00:01:09.748
architects can't be the queue for every design.

00:01:10.448 --> 00:01:15.811
NEXUS is built to make designs compliant from the first draft, find failures

00:01:15.811 --> 00:01:21.597
before anything is built, and let every architect design safely, inside the firm's

00:01:21.597 --> 00:01:22.373
guardrails.

00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:29.913
Here's the idea. NEXUS designs within the firm's guardrails, then breaks the design

00:01:29.913 --> 00:01:34.497
on purpose. Zone A fails, and the surviving zone is overloaded.

00:01:35.197 --> 00:01:39.835
A fix is proposed, by an agent, or by you. An architect approves

00:01:39.835 --> 00:01:44.328
it, and the design is broken again, to prove that it survives.

00:01:46.826 --> 00:01:51.414
Let's do it for real, from a blank canvas. I'll describe the system

00:01:51.414 --> 00:01:57.303
in plain language: an online banking portal for three hundred thousand customers, that

00:01:57.303 --> 00:01:59.221
must survive a zone failure.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.305
The local model doesn't guess. It asks for what it needs: the platform,

00:02:04.305 --> 00:02:08.133
the peak load, and whether the data is sensitive. I'll answer.

00:02:08.832 --> 00:02:13.320
It returns a plan, not a change. The Action Guard checks the plan

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:19.328
against the firm's ground truth, principles and approved technology, before I apply it.

00:02:20.029 --> 00:02:26.218
Here's the draft: CDN, WAF, gateway, three services, events, cache and databases. The

00:02:26.218 --> 00:02:32.189
Design Advisor lists what still needs attention, and every finding cites its rule.

00:02:32.889 --> 00:02:38.588
Guardrails aren't suggestions. Watch what happens if I try to remove the web

00:02:38.588 --> 00:02:45.261
application firewall. Ground truth blocks it: internet facing endpoints must sit behind a

00:02:45.261 --> 00:02:45.561
WAF.

00:02:46.261 --> 00:02:51.456
I'll ask for a second opinion. A larger model reviews the same facts,

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:57.328
and suggests keeping the WAF, and rate limiting the gateway instead. NEXUS dry

00:02:57.328 --> 00:03:02.597
runs that option with the same rules, so its numbers are computed, not

00:03:02.597 --> 00:03:03.200
claimed.

00:03:03.899 --> 00:03:08.268
This is a key decision, so a person makes it. I'll choose opinion

00:03:08.268 --> 00:03:13.243
two. It's recorded as an architecture decision, with both sets of numbers.

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:19.193
Now, the interactive test. I'll push the load to a thousand times today's

00:03:19.193 --> 00:03:25.522
traffic. Services autoscale, the cost follows, and NEXUS names the component that breaks

00:03:25.522 --> 00:03:25.954
first.

00:03:26.653 --> 00:03:32.993
Next, chaos engineering. I'll fail availability zone A. Thirty percent of requests fail:

00:03:32.993 --> 00:03:37.460
the cache and the event bus each ran a single instance, in the

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.261
zone that just went down.

00:03:39.961 --> 00:03:45.507
The Design Advisor proposes a fix for each. I'll apply them: two instances,

00:03:45.507 --> 00:03:50.535
spread across zones. With zone A still down, the error rate drops to

00:03:50.535 --> 00:03:50.905
zero.

00:03:51.605 --> 00:03:57.161
Now a bigger blast radius. Two regions, active active, and region A fails.

00:03:57.161 --> 00:04:02.491
Half the users fail: there's no DNS steering, and the databases live in

00:04:02.491 --> 00:04:03.317
one region.

00:04:04.016 --> 00:04:09.044
Choosing the multi region database is a decision with real trade offs, so

00:04:09.044 --> 00:04:14.555
NEXUS compares the options, with their constraints, before an architect decides.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:20.944
The same design runs on any platform. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, or your

00:04:20.944 --> 00:04:25.111
own data centre, and hybrid patterns that span both.

00:04:25.811 --> 00:04:33.318
Every change regenerates infrastructure as code. The Terraform runs with Terraform or OpenTofu,

00:04:33.318 --> 00:04:35.688
and it's never edited by hand.

00:04:36.388 --> 00:04:40.697
And the same model generates the architecture in LikeC4.

00:04:43.196 --> 00:04:49.334
LikeC4 renders it as interactive C 4 diagrams, from context down to components,

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:52.753
so the diagrams never drift from the design.

00:04:55.254 --> 00:05:00.937
That's NEXUS. Design inside your guardrails, break it before production does, and keep

00:05:00.937 --> 00:05:03.382
every key decision with an architect.

00:05:04.082 --> 00:05:09.138
NEXUS is coming soon. Please contact me to know more. Thanks for watching.
